I wrote `review-unfamiliar.json` to the Paul Bettany run folder, and it passes the review schema check. Each finding and protected passage matches the draft word for word. I didn't touch any other file or publish anything.

**Overall:** someone who has never heard of Bettany can follow this draft. The 9/11 opening grabs attention, the type answer is plain, and the early sections each add something new. The problems are mostly about order and missing context, not the substance.

**Two major findings (the editor has to fix these):**
- **The 2003–2014 career years can't be put in order.** The section calls *Margin Call* (2011) "the turn," then jumps back to "after the 2008 crash." The Vision offer has no date. It also never says he was already voicing J.A.R.V.I.S. when Whedon called, so "Marvel became the floor" comes out of nowhere. I opened the 2022 Daily Beast interview to check the order. It has the missing payoff: Whedon got him Vision by showing one of the money films Bettany thought was "the nadir of my career."
- **One Depp quote is paraphrased into a timing claim it doesn't make.** He said "we hadn't spoken for years," and the draft adds "by the time Depp married Heard." That sits badly with the texts from 2013. The draft also quotes only the middle of his 2016 tweet. The full tweet opens "Known Johnny Depp for years and through several relationships," which shows it was a public defense of Depp. The article leaves this contradiction unflagged in its one section about abuse allegations.

**Six minor findings:**
- **Depp section:** "Amber" appears before the reader learns who she is. The libel case is never explained. "Depp's reply went further" hints at something and never says what.
- **"Counterphobic":** the term is defined, then never used. Meanwhile a frightened boy taking beatings he could have avoided goes unexplained.
- **The mask line:** the title's central idea ("a profession he describes as a mask") hangs on an "also" that doesn't connect to anything before it.
- **The father section:** it says the father's reasons are "not on record," then gives him a motive two paragraphs later.
- **First reason against Type 4:** "under pressure" versus "on purpose, as a plan" won't make sense to a reader who doesn't know the Enneagram.
- **Depp section placement:** it sits right before the ending and stalls it. The body is also slightly over the length target, at about 3,550 words against 3,500.

**Five passages to keep:** the silent crowd at the tavern, "That confession holds the whole pattern…", the John Lennon comparison, "Paul tells his children authenticity is worth what it costs, and keeps a mask for himself," and the closing "I'm just better at hiding it" line.

The duplicate-wording check against other recent profiles didn't trigger. I compared the closest matches (the Christopher Abbott and Josh Hartnett drafts) and the overlap is only common Type 6 vocabulary.
